---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import NewsletterEmbed from '../components/NewsletterEmbed.astro';

// Scoped styles for elements not covered by the global article.css
// (body lists, inline links, figures, references, disclaimer).
const articleStyles = `
    .article-body a {
        color: var(--accent);
        text-decoration: underline;
        text-underline-offset: 2px;
        text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
        transition: color var(--duration-short) ease;
    }
    .article-body a:hover { color: var(--text); }
    .article-body ul, .article-body ol {
        margin: 0 0 20px;
        padding-left: 22px;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .article-body li {
        font-size: 16px;
        line-height: 1.75;
        margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .article-body ul { list-style: disc; }
    .article-body ol { list-style: decimal; }
    .article-body strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
    .article-body sup a { text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.7em; }
    figure.fig { margin: var(--sp-8) 0; }
    figure.fig > img {
        width: 100%;
        display: block;
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    }
    .schema-wrap {
        background: #faf9f7;
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
        padding: 20px;
    }
    .schema-wrap img { width: 100%; display: block; }
    figure.fig figcaption {
        font-size: 13px;
        color: var(--text-tertiary);
        margin-top: 10px;
        text-align: center;
        line-height: 1.5;
    }
    .arc-flow {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-items: stretch;
        gap: 10px;
        margin: var(--sp-6) 0 var(--sp-8);
    }
    .arc-step {
        flex: 1 1 160px;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        font-family: var(--font-body);
        font-size: 14px;
        font-weight: 500;
        color: var(--text);
        background: var(--bg-subtle);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius-md, 12px);
        padding: 12px 16px;
        line-height: 1.35;
    }
    .arc-arrow {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        color: var(--accent);
        font-size: 16px;
        font-weight: 600;
        line-height: 1;
    }
    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .arc-flow { flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; }
        .arc-step { flex: none; }
        .arc-arrow { justify-content: center; transform: rotate(90deg); }
    }
    .disclaimer {
        font-size: 14px;
        color: var(--text-tertiary);
        font-style: italic;
        margin-top: var(--sp-8);
        line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .ref-list { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; }
    .ref-list li { margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--text-secondary); }
    .cta-box {
        margin: var(--sp-12) 0;
        padding: var(--sp-8) var(--sp-6);
        background: var(--bg-subtle);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
        text-align: center;
    }
    .cta-box p {
        margin: 0 0 var(--sp-6);
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        font-size: 15px;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .cta-button {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 8px;
        background: var(--text);
        color: var(--bg) !important;
        font-family: var(--font-body);
        font-size: 15px;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: 0.01em;
        padding: 13px 26px;
        border-radius: var(--radius-full);
        text-decoration: none !important;
        transition: opacity var(--duration-short) ease;
    }
    .cta-button:hover { opacity: 0.85; }
    .cta-repo {
        display: block;
        margin-top: var(--sp-4);
        font-size: 13px;
        color: var(--text-tertiary);
    }
`;
---
<ArticleLayout
    title="My Health OS: all my health data in one place, read by AI"
    description="Bloodwork, wearables, genome, all in one folder. Here's how to build your Health Operating System."
    ogUrl="https://maxguerois.com/health-os"
    ogImage="https://maxguerois.com/health-journey/banner-health-os.jpg"
    articleStyles={articleStyles}
>

<div class="container">
    <a href="/newsletter" class="back">← Back to the newsletter</a>

    <div class="article-meta">June 2026</div>
    <h1 class="article-title">My Health OS: all my health data in one place, read by AI</h1>
    <p class="article-subtitle">Bloodwork, wearables, genome, all in one folder. Here's how to build your Health Operating System.</p>

    <img
        class="article-img"
        src="/health-journey/banner-health-os.jpg"
        alt="My Health OS: all my health data in one place, read by AI"
        fetchpriority="high"
    >

    <div class="article-body">

        <p>After the genome issue, a lot of you wanted the same thing, and it wasn't the genome. It was the folder I dropped it into: my Health OS. "How do I build one?"</p>

        <p>My friend Max Barbier didn't wait for instructions. He built his own in a day:</p>

        <figure class="fig">
            <img
                src="/health-journey/max-barbier-conversation.png"
                alt="Max Barbier messaging Max Guerois that he built his own Health OS in a day"
                loading="lazy"
            >
        </figure>

        <p>So here's where it came from, and how to build yours.</p>

        <p>It started with one tweet. In April, Andrej Karpathy posted about a plain-markdown "LLM wiki"<sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup>. It clicked: this was how I'd finally make sense of my scattered health data.</p>

        <p>Bryan Johnson built his own off the same post<sup><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup>. When he and a guy in Paris build the same thing off one tweet, that's where this is going.</p>

        <p>By the end, you'll have your own.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">What a Health OS is</h2>

        <p>It's simple, almost stupidly so.</p>

        <p>A folder on your computer, one plain text file per part of your health: bloodwork, wearables, genome, family history.</p>

        <p>And you keep adding to it. Every new lab, every month of wearable data stacks on top of the last. The files aren't a snapshot. They're a record that gets longer and richer over time.</p>

        <p>The folder lives on your machine. You point an AI at it whenever you want, and it reads across all of it at once, the whole history, every source, and turns it into decisions.</p>

        <p>No app, no dashboard, no code.</p>

        <figure class="fig">
            <div class="schema-wrap">
                <img
                    src="/health-journey/health-os-schema.png"
                    alt="Your health files go in, the AI reads across all of them, decisions come out"
                    loading="lazy"
                >
            </div>
            <figcaption>Your files go in, the AI reads across them, decisions come out.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 class="section-heading">I had years of data. Nothing read it together.</h2>

        <p>For years it piled up, in two speeds.</p>

        <p>The daily stuff: Whoop on my sleep and recovery, Withings on my weight, Strava on every run and workout.</p>

        <p>And the deep tests, more like once a year: bloodwork from Lucis (the company I built), a DEXA scan, my genome, my family's panels.</p>

        <p>Good data, all of it. But each source only ever saw its own slice.</p>

        <p>My Whoop didn't know my ferritin was low. My bloodwork didn't know I'd trained hard all week. Nothing looked at the whole thing.</p>

        <p>That's the gap an LLM closes.</p>

        <p>It reads across plain, messy files from every source and finds what no single one can see on its own. No database, no integrations.</p>

        <p>You hand it your text, and it reasons over all of it at once. The data you already have is enough. It just needs one reader that sees all of it.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">Then I made it update itself</h2>

        <p>I started simple. Dumped everything into the folder and started asking questions. It worked right away, ask anything, get an answer across all my data at once. Things no single app could see.</p>

        <p>But I wanted to push it further. The folder was already great. It got better the more current it stayed, and the moment it updated itself daily, it started to compound.</p>

        <p>The friction was keeping it current by hand. Every Sunday I'd sit down to plan my training, and first I had to:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>pull in my latest data myself, Whoop, Strava, Withings</li>
            <li>merge it into everything else</li>
            <li><em>then</em> actually plan</li>
        </ul>

        <p>It worked. But it ate time, every single week.</p>

        <p>So I automated it.</p>

        <p>And here's the part I still can't quite believe: I didn't write a single line of it. I told Claude Code what I wanted, and it built the whole thing, the scripts, the integrations, all of it.</p>

        <p>Now it runs on its own:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Whoop, Withings, and Strava flow in automatically</li>
            <li>it enriches itself, spots trends, predicts on fresh data instead of a stale snapshot</li>
            <li>the only thing I still add by hand is the deep tests, and those come once a year anyway</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Then I took it one step further. I had Claude Code wire it into a Telegram bot, Max Health Coach. Now it comes to me:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>every morning, on its own</li>
            <li>my readiness, the workout for the day, a nudge when something's off</li>
            <li>I don't open a folder or ask anymore. It just shows up.</li>
        </ul>

        <figure class="fig">
            <img
                src="/health-journey/max-health-coach-telegram.png"
                alt="Max Health Coach messaging me every morning on Telegram with my readiness and workout"
                loading="lazy"
            >
        </figure>

        <p>That's the whole arc:</p>

        <div class="arc-flow">
            <span class="arc-step">a folder I query</span>
            <span class="arc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="arc-step">a system that updates itself</span>
            <span class="arc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="arc-step">a coach that texts me every morning</span>
        </div>

        <p>And I never touched the code.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">Why you'd build one too</h2>

        <p>A few months in, here's what it actually buys me.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Safer decisions.</strong> Before anything new goes in my body, a peptide, a supplement, a new protocol, I run it against my own data first. I did exactly that before starting retatrutide, the GLP-1 I'm experimenting with.</li>
            <li><strong>Genuinely data-driven.</strong> I can ask "how is my retatrutide experiment actually going?" and it reads across everything at once, the markers I watch daily, resting heart rate, HRV (how much the time between my heartbeats varies, a good read on recovery), weight, plus my training and bloodwork. The data tells me, not a hunch.</li>
            <li><strong>Time and money saved.</strong> And honestly, it's made me lean on a doctor less for the day-to-day. Not for anything that genuinely needs one, but the small "should I tweak this?" calls I used to book an appointment for, I just ask my own data now.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>If you measure anything about your health, this is what turns the numbers into decisions.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">How to set it up, no code</h2>

        <p>You don't need the automation to start, that came later. If you can edit a text file and paste a prompt, you can do this.</p>

        <p><strong>1. Pick a tool that reads a folder.</strong> Claude (Cowork or Claude Code), OpenAI Codex, any AI you can point at a folder. Free tiers are fine.</p>

        <p><strong>2. Make the folder.</strong> One plain file per topic:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>profile.md</strong>: you, your goals, your targets</li>
            <li><strong>data/</strong>: your raw results, bloodwork, wearables, body comp, genetics</li>
            <li><strong>protocol/</strong>: what you're doing, supplements, training</li>
            <li><strong>instructions.md</strong>: the file that turns the AI into your coach</li>
            <li><strong>dashboard.md</strong>: a snapshot it keeps updated</li>
        </ul>

        <figure class="fig">
            <div class="schema-wrap">
                <img
                    src="/health-journey/health-os-template-structure.png"
                    alt="The Health OS Starter template file structure: profile, data, protocol, instructions, and dashboard files"
                    loading="lazy"
                >
            </div>
            <figcaption>The Health OS Starter template structure.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p><strong>3. Add your data.</strong> Paste in what you have, lab PDFs, your Whoop or Oura, Apple Health, a DEXA. Start with bloodwork and one wearable, add the rest later.</p>

        <p><strong>4. Give it the coach instructions.</strong> This file does the work: read everything first, never invent a number, cite sources, flag anything clinical. Ask for your read and it tells you where you stand, what's flagged, what to do, what to stop, what's missing. It plans and coaches too.</p>

        <p><strong>5. Run it weekly.</strong> New data lands, drop it in, say "give me my read." The picture compounds. Data you collect is a curiosity. Data something acts on every week is a coach.</p>

        <div class="cta-box">
            <p>I packaged the exact scaffold, the coach prompt, and example (fake) data into a free starter template. Copy it, swap in your data, point your AI at it.</p>
            <a class="cta-button" href="https://github.com/gm-max/health-os-starter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get the Health OS Starter →</a>
            <span class="cta-repo">github.com/gm-max/health-os-starter</span>
        </div>

        <p><a href="/my-genome">The genome</a> was the base layer. This is the thing that finally acts on it.</p>

        <p>If you build one, reply and tell me what you're tracking. I read every reply, and I'll help you get yours set up.</p>

        <p>More soon,<br>Max</p>

        <p class="disclaimer">Personal experimentation and documentation, not medical advice. Talk to a doctor before making health decisions.</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">References</h2>

        <ol class="ref-list">
            <li id="ref-1"><a href="https://x.com/karpathy/status/2039805659525644595" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrej Karpathy on the "LLM wiki" (plain-markdown second brain for LLMs)</a></li>
            <li id="ref-2"><a href="https://x.com/bryan_johnson/status/2045962306291699907" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bryan Johnson on building his own health database</a></li>
        </ol>

    </div>

    <div class="article-footer">
        <span class="author-line">By <a href="/">Max Guerois</a></span>
    </div>

    <!-- Newsletter CTA -->
    <div style="margin-top: 48px;">
        <NewsletterEmbed />
    </div>
</div>
</ArticleLayout>
